window.COACH = {"page": "entrepreneurship", "title": "Entrepreneurship", "prizeName": "Best Entrepreneurship", "summary": {"verdict": "This is a real business plan, not a sketch: the six-year roadmap, the regulatory comparison, the nine risks with mitigations and the stakeholder grid already answer more of the ballot than two of the seven winning pages did. What holds it back is not missing work but disagreement inside the work. Prices, dates, markets, SDG numbers, the third protein and the kill switch each appear in two or three different versions across this page and the Laws, Human Practices, Sustainability and Geospatial pages, and a judge who notices one stops trusting the rest.", "strengths": ["The regulatory comparison in section 7: two routes, their cost in time, the choice, and what is deferred.", "The roadmap in section 6.1 is text, with durations and done counts, so a judge can audit it. Several winners buried theirs in images.", "Honest sentences about your own product: it will not beat the best chemicals, the B. subtilis survival rate may be too low, Ms. Chen's objection that plants may stop building their own resilience.", "Stakeholder entries that say what changed, especially dropping price competition with chemical fertilizers after Professor Chen's advice.", "Risk mitigations written as tasks someone performs on a schedule, like the monthly membrane check with extension officers."], "priorities": ["Fix the price. Pick one number and one currency, and make section 2.1, the Our solution box, section 6.2 and the Geospatial page's NT$30,000 agree.", "Fix the contradictions with other ReLeaf pages: the contained-use pathway against Laws and Regulations, the kill switch against Human Practices, the SDG numbers against Sustainability.", "Decide who buys first. Conventional smallholders in section 5.4 and organic farms in the obtainable market cannot both be the beachhead, and no first customer is named anywhere.", "Add the two missing ballot answers: your team's own skills and gaps, and the negative long-term impacts of the finished product.", "Make one decision per claim and delete the repeats: the mission appears three times, the PESTEL paragraphs restate the chart, and the value proposition sentence is used twice."], "ballot": [{"question": "Has the team identified their first potential customers and their unmet need(s) that are not yet covered by existing solutions?", "status": "partial", "where": "4. Market analysis (Stakeholder analysis, Key stakeholders)", "note": "Eight stakeholder groups and six consultations are documented, but no first customer is named, nobody is quoted saying what they would buy, and the unmet need is never written as a list of conditions the product must meet."}, {"question": "Has the team shown that their solution is possible, scalable and inventive?", "status": "partial", "where": "3. Business model (SWOT), 4. Market analysis (Strategic positioning, Competitor analysis), 6.1 Product development plan", "note": "Scale-up is planned unit by unit and the MVP figure appears twice, but MVP is never defined, the first-on-the-market claim carries no hedge or search, and the competitor table has slipped out of alignment."}, {"question": "Has the team presented logical product development plans with realistic milestones, timelines, resources and risks?", "status": "strong", "where": "6.1 Product development plan, 5.6 Marketing timeline, 6.2 Financial projection, 8. Potential risks", "note": "The strongest part of the page. Weakened by the 2026 against 2027 pilot dates, three different expansion orders, and the fixed cost that changes between the table and the break-even sum."}, {"question": "Has the team outlined the skills, capabilities, and the stakeholders required to be credible in developing their solution further?", "status": "partial", "where": "4. Market analysis (Key stakeholders), 3. Business model (Key partners, Key resources)", "note": "Stakeholders are well documented. The team's own skills, gaps and who would fill them are not mentioned anywhere, which is the cheapest missing section on the page."}, {"question": "Has the team considered the positive and negative long-term impacts of their fully developed solution?", "status": "partial", "where": "3. Business model (SWOT weaknesses and threats), 8. Potential risks, 9. Conclusion", "note": "Negatives exist as present-day risks, but the long-term view in the conclusion is all positive. No negative impact of the finished product is paired with a cause and a mitigation."}], "skeleton": {"releaf": ["1. Overview", "2. Product description", "3. Business model", "4. Market analysis", "5. Marketing strategy", "6. Future plans", "7. Regulations", "8. Potential risks", "9. Conclusion", "References"], "winners": ["Problem, with sourced numbers", "Unmet need named as a gap in one sentence", "Customers, from a class down to a named first customer", "Solution and why it is inventive, against named competitors", "Market size, TAM to SAM to SOM with the arithmetic shown", "Competitors, direct, indirect and future", "Money: costs, revenue model, funding plan", "Timeline with milestones", "Risks as phase, risk and response", "Skills and stakeholders", "Long-term impacts, positive and negative", "Downloads: business plan, pitch deck, financials"], "gap": "Five of the seven winners build their headings out of the five ballot questions, so a judge reaches each answer in one scroll. ReLeaf uses a consulting structure instead, which is what the two weakest winning pages did, and it hides three answers: the first customer sits inside Market analysis, the skills answer is missing, and the long-term negatives are scattered through SWOT and the risk table. Your order is also inside out at the start, with the product in section 1 and the problem folded away."}, "extras": ["Spelling: change “miniscule” to “minuscule” (section 1, climate box).", "“Maintenance of the bioreactor is considerably minimal” does not make sense. Change it to “The bioreactor needs very little maintenance” (section 2.2).", "“bioreactor repairment” is not a word. Change it to “bioreactor repairs” (section 2.2, The App).", "Change “distinct from an Non-Governmental Organization (NGO)” to “different from a non-governmental organization (NGO)” (section 3.1).", "“safety measures that reduce safety and regulatory concerns” says you reduce safety. Change it to “safety measures that reduce biosafety risks and regulatory concerns” (SWOT, Strengths).", "The PESTEL intro stops at “legal lens we can see that” and never finishes. Change it to “Fig. 4 looks at our project through six lenses: political, economic, social, technological, environmental and legal.”", "The same sentence appears twice: “Overall, our system gives farmers an automatic, customizable, more effective, and sustainable way...” at the end of the canvas, and “Our system offers farmers an automatic, customizable, more effective, and sustainable way...” under Value proposition. Delete the first one.", "Change “It also takes into great consideration of biosafety” to “It is also designed for biosafety” (Our solution box).", "Tense: change “only 3.47% of Taiwan’s total farmland is organic and eco-friendly by 2024” to “only 3.47% of Taiwan’s farmland was organic or eco-friendly in 2024”.", "Change “which earning their adoption would serve as an additional point of validation” to “and earning their trust would add credibility to our project” (Sustainable farming farmers).", "Change “a Taiwan commercial indoor vertical farm” to “a Taiwanese commercial indoor vertical farm” (Yes Health iFarm).", "Change “which they hold practical knowledge of purchasing behavior” to “so they know about purchasing behavior” (Agriculture distributor).", "Two sentences joined by a comma. Change “agricultural standards, a single policy shift can make or break” to “agricultural standards. A single policy change can make or break” (Government).", "Change “keep track with them” to “keep track of them” (World Vegetable Center).", "One company, two spellings: “Agritalk” in the stakeholder grid and “AgriTalk” in the competitor summary. Check the company’s own spelling and use it in both places.", "“collabs” is slang. Change “potential collabs” to “possible collaborations” (Key companies box).", "Grammar: change “the interest in bioprotectants have also increased” to “interest in bioprotectants has also increased” (Market opportunities box).", "“unlike current solutions which require farmers to constantly have to interpret” never finishes its comparison. Make it its own sentence: “Current solutions make farmers decide when, how much and where to apply protectants, which risks overuse or underuse.”", "“leasing middlemen” means renting people. Change it to “working through distributors who sell our product to farmers” (section 5.3).", "Fig. 9 is described as a “Barriers to entry table”, but there is no table or image, only four text blocks. Either add the table or delete the caption.", "Grammar: change “can supports” to “can support” (section 5.6).", "Change “allowing others to gain accessibility on our product” to “so that academic labs can buy and use our product” (Sales and Partnerships).", "The roadmap caption “ReLeaf Strategic Master Roadmap. Unified 6-year Gantt chart.” has no figure number, between Fig. 10 and Fig. 11. Number it and renumber the figures after it.", "The roadmap marks Year 1 “8/8 done”, but the Year 1 text says “the team will demonstrate”. If it is done, write “demonstrated”; if not, mark the rows as planned.", "Change “pilot success metrics would be done with deploying about 20 to 50 prototype devices” to “in the field pilot we will deploy 20 to 50 prototype devices” (Year 2).", "Phase numbers do not match. The Year 3 text says “phase 3 marks the transition from local to industrial scale”, but the roadmap labels scale-up “2Industrial scale-up” (and the Taiwan phase “1.2”). Use the same numbers in the text and the chart.", "Change “Philippine is a promising market” to “The Philippines is a promising market” (Year 3).", "Tense: change “is intensively managed and relied heavily” to “is intensively managed and relies heavily” (Year 3, Vietnam).", "Year 4 says “The final phase focuses on deploying 500 to 1500 units”, but the roadmap has another phase after it (Global market expansion). Change “The final phase” to “This phase”.", "Packaging has prices ($0.5 and $0.25 per unit) but is marked “N/A” and left out of the “Total variable cost $201.55”. Add it ($202.30) or say why packaging is not counted.", "Two sentences joined by a comma. Change “not just for the Jamboree, we want to develop” to “not just for the Jamboree. We want to develop” (Stage 2).", "Change “a evidence of crop benefit” to “evidence of crop benefit” (Stage 2).", "The letters in “Animal and Plant Health Inspection Agency (BAPHIQ)” do not match the name. Write the agency’s official English name in full and check that the acronym matches it (section 7)."]}, "notes": [{"id": "ent-001", "type": "restructure", "anchor": "ReLeaf is an optogenetic bioreactor system that enables automated, on-demand protection", "title": "Start with the farmer's problem", "body": "The page opens with your machine. Your best numbers about crop loss sit in the folded box below, so a judge who does not open the fold never sees why this matters. Move one sourced sentence up here, then say what ReLeaf is.", "after": "Heat and salt already cause about half of the world's crop losses each year (Bray et al., 2000). ReLeaf is a bioreactor that makes crop protectants on the farm, only when sensors show stress.", "excerpts": ["ent-2023-og-leiden-31"], "excerpt_note": "Leiden gives the number, the harm and the gap in four sentences before naming its own product.", "ballot": "Has the team identified their first potential customers and their unmet need(s) that are not yet covered by existing solutions?"}, {"id": "ent-002", "type": "mistake", "anchor": "automatically produces and releases protective proteins directly to the crop root zone", "title": "Root zone or spraying, pick one", "body": "Here the protectant goes into the root zone through irrigation. In section 2.2 it is “a cell-free liquid that the farmer can then use to spray on their crops”, and Fig. 2 step 3 says “spray in fields”. A judge will see two different products. Decide which one you built and use it everywhere.", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "ent-003", "type": "mistake", "anchor": "LEA 14 (abiotic stress protection), ACCD (ethylene reduction), and NaD1 (antifungal defense)", "title": "Third protein has two names", "body": "The antifungal protein is NaD1 here and in Stage 1 of the funding plan, but BoPep4 in the Year 1 roadmap and in the summary at the end of section 6.1. The Laws and Regulations page uses only BoPep4. Pick one name for this page and say in one clause how it relates to the other, if they are related.", "excerpts": ["ent-2025-ug-sustech-16"], "excerpt_note": "A prize-winning page let one organism carry several names in one block. It is the first thing a careful reader notices."}, {"id": "ent-004", "type": "cut", "anchor": "Mission statement: ReLeaf strengthens agricultural resilience by enabling automated, on-demand protection", "title": "Mission repeats the first paragraph", "body": "This sentence says the same thing as the first two sentences of the Overview, in almost the same words. Keep one of them.", "after": "Delete this line and keep the Overview paragraph, which already states the mission.", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "ent-005", "type": "praise", "anchor": "Vision: Every farmer is a biomanufacturer.", "title": "Five words a judge will remember", "body": "This is the strongest line on the page. It states your ambition in five plain words, and the sentence after it explains what that means in practice. Keep it exactly as it is.", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "ent-006", "type": "cut", "anchor": "humanity is forced to choose between immediate technological adaptation or multi-system crop failure", "title": "Drop the dramatic opener", "body": "The sentences after this one carry real numbers with sources, and they are much stronger. This opening clause makes the reader wait for them.", "after": "In a changing climate, seasons shift, temperatures swing, soil salinity rises and fungal outbreaks grow worse.", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "ent-007", "type": "restructure", "anchor": "compounding cellular damage in ways that can’t be triggered by either stress independently", "title": "Say caused, not triggered", "body": "As written this says the damage cannot be triggered, which is the opposite of your point. One word changes the meaning.", "after": "compounding cellular damage in ways that neither stress causes on its own", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "ent-008", "type": "mistake", "anchor": "The price is about 100,000 to 150,000 in total for initial installation", "title": "Four different prices, no currency", "body": "The number above this line is 135,297.77 with no currency. This sentence says 100,000 to 150,000, also with no currency. In the Our solution box you write “$60,000 NTD”, and the Geospatial Analysis page builds its whole cost model on “unit cost (NT$30,000)”. Agree on one price and one currency with the Geospatial team, then write it the same way in all four places.", "after": "One ReLeaf unit costs about NT$[number] to install. Prices vary with tiered pricing and subsidies.", "excerpts": [], "ballot": "Has the team presented logical product development plans with realistic milestones, timelines, resources and risks?"}, {"id": "ent-009", "type": "praise", "anchor": "The system has three core components that work together to monitor field conditions", "title": "Good signpost before three parts", "body": "This sentence tells the reader what is coming, and the next three paragraphs deliver the Sensors, the Bioreactor and the App in that order. A judge can follow the product without a diagram.", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "ent-010", "type": "restructure", "anchor": "Consult with someone from our team/company to see the soil quality and condition of your farm", "title": "Shorten the first setup step", "body": "Step 1 is one 40-word sentence with “to see” twice. Shorter steps are easier for a farmer, and for a judge reading quickly.", "after": "Ask our team to survey your farm. We check how many soil types you have and whether conditions change from one area to another.", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "ent-011", "type": "clarify", "anchor": "If you add the our line channel, you will be able to send messages/requests", "title": "Fix the typo and explain LINE", "body": "“add the our line channel” has an extra word. An international judge also will not know that LINE is the messaging app almost everyone in Taiwan uses, or what the Chinese account name means.", "after": "You can also add our LINE account (LINE is the main messaging app in Taiwan) and send us questions or requests. Account name: 農友助手 (Farmer's Helper).", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "ent-012", "type": "restructure", "anchor": "What they do is instead of making farmers have to go through middlemen in order to sell their product", "title": "Split this spoken sentence", "body": "This sentence runs for about 60 words and holds three ideas, so the reader loses the thread. Say one thing per sentence.", "after": "USE-GH buys crops from farmers directly, at a much lower fee than middlemen charge. It also teaches farmers to process their raw materials into finished products, so they earn more from the same harvest.", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "ent-013", "type": "praise", "anchor": "Imperfections in the bioreactor could lead to dilution of the protectants, while biofouling inside the chambers may reduce protein yield", "title": "Weaknesses name real mechanisms", "body": "You name how the product could fail, not just that it might: dilution, biofouling, a blocked valve. That is far more convincing than a careful sentence about risk, and it tells a judge you understand your own hardware.", "excerpts": ["ent-2025-ug-sustech-15"], "excerpt_note": "SUSTech-BIO writes each weakness as a process that goes wrong, in one line each."}, {"id": "ent-014", "type": "mistake", "anchor": "The global push to meet UN Sustainability Goals (particularly SDGs 2, 8, 11, 12, 13, and 15)", "title": "SDG numbers clash with Sustainability", "body": "This page claims SDGs 2, 8, 11, 12, 13 and 15, and the SDG box lower down repeats them. The Sustainability page chooses SDG 2, 4, 10 and 15, and says plainly that Climate Action “isn’t the main point of our project”. Two pages cannot pick different goals. Use the Sustainability page's four and link to it.", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "ent-015", "type": "cut", "anchor": "Legal and regulatory frameworks also require a lot of careful navigation", "title": "Six PESTEL paragraphs, one job", "body": "All six paragraphs open the same way and mostly restate the chart above them. Keep the two that change a decision (the GMO rules and the subsidy route) and delete the rest. The page is long, and this is the cheapest place to shorten it.", "excerpts": ["ent-2024-ug-padua-21"], "excerpt_note": "A winner shipped two marketing sections that opened with the same paragraph. Read your opening sentences in a column and delete the repeats."}, {"id": "ent-016", "type": "clarify", "anchor": "Operating under a social enterprise model, we maintain an accessible, small basic fee structure", "title": "The business has no name here", "body": "The canvas figure calls the company GEMS Taiwan, but the text only ever says ReLeaf. A judge cannot tell whether ReLeaf is the product, the team or the company. Say it once, early in section 3.", "after": "GEMS Taiwan is the social enterprise we would register; ReLeaf is the product it sells.", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "ent-017", "type": "clarify", "anchor": "By utilizing our optimized CcaS-CcaR two-component optogenetic system linked to automated LED arrays", "title": "Gloss the light system once", "body": "A business reader meets CcaS-CcaR here with no explanation. One short clause is enough, and the rest of the paragraph already says what it does.", "after": "Green and red LEDs switch protein production on and off through our light-sensing system (CcaS-CcaR).", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "ent-018", "type": "restructure", "anchor": "A traditional business model canvas focuses on single value exchange", "title": "This paragraph belongs one section up", "body": "This explains the social business model canvas, which you presented in section 3. Sitting under Market analysis, it reads as if the canvas is about to start again. Move it to the top of the canvas section in 3.", "after": "Move this paragraph so it opens the Social business model canvas section, before Key partners.", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "ent-019", "type": "praise", "anchor": "are not the same person who benefits from the mission", "title": "Payer and beneficiary split in one line", "body": "This is the clearest business sentence on the page. It explains in one line why you need a social enterprise and why the canvas has two sides. Everything in section 3 follows from it.", "excerpts": ["ent-2023-ug-tec-27"], "excerpt_note": "Tec-Chihuahua splits one customer group by who actually pays, then names the buyer that creates."}, {"id": "ent-020", "type": "praise", "anchor": "only 3.47% of Taiwan’s total farmland is organic and eco-friendly by 2024", "title": "A number that changes your claim", "body": "You give the figure, name the agency, add the global number, and then draw the consequence: without conventional farmers your market shrinks. That is the shape a judge trusts, because the number does work in the argument.", "excerpts": ["ent-2025-ug-sustech-12"], "excerpt_note": "Each claim carries its source in the same sentence, with a comparison that sizes it for an outsider."}, {"id": "ent-021", "type": "suggest", "anchor": "Helped us better understand what it’s like for small farmers that face climate stress", "title": "Name your first customer", "body": "The ballot asks who would buy first. This section lists eight stakeholders but never says which one would place the first order, or what they said they would need before paying. Add a short block here called First potential customers.", "after": "First potential customers. [Name], [role] at [organisation], told us they would trial [number] units if [condition]. We contacted them on [date].", "excerpts": ["ent-2025-og-wur-01", "ent-2024-og-utoronto-23"], "excerpt_note": "Both winners name the company, say what it told them, and say who contacted whom.", "ballot": "Has the team identified their first potential customers and their unmet need(s) that are not yet covered by existing solutions?"}, {"id": "ent-022", "type": "praise", "anchor": "Realized it’s not possible to compete price-wise with chemical fertilizers so we removed that from our business plan", "title": "An expert changed your plan", "body": "This is the most valuable sentence in the stakeholder section. An expert told you something, and you dropped a claim because of it. Keep writing changes this plainly.", "excerpts": ["ent-2023-og-leiden-32"], "excerpt_note": "Leiden says stakeholders talked it out of its own plan, then shows the new plan.", "ballot": "Has the team outlined the skills, capabilities, and the stakeholders required to be credible in developing their solution further?"}, {"id": "ent-023", "type": "suggest", "anchor": "Advised that we research more about regulations in Taiwan and any country we plan to expand to before actually taking action.", "title": "Two entries are missing their change", "body": "The CH Biotech and World Vegetable Centre entries stop at the advice. Four of your six entries have a “What we changed” line, which is what a judge is looking for. Add the missing two, even if the change was small or you decided not to act.", "after": "What we changed. Started a country-by-country regulations table for Taiwan and each expansion market, which became section 7.", "excerpts": ["ent-2025-og-wur-01"], "ballot": "Has the team outlined the skills, capabilities, and the stakeholders required to be credible in developing their solution further?"}, {"id": "ent-024", "type": "praise", "anchor": "Ms. Chen also emphasized the importance of addressing the concern that providing plants with external support", "title": "You recorded a criticism of the product", "body": "Ms. Chen's point cuts against your whole idea, and you wrote it down anyway and added it to your weaknesses. Judges read that as honesty, and it makes your positive claims easier to believe.", "excerpts": ["ihp-2024-og-uzh-02"], "excerpt_note": "A GMO critic is quoted fairly and her concerns become testing obligations.", "ballot": "Has the team considered the positive and negative long-term impacts of their fully developed solution?"}, {"id": "ent-025", "type": "restructure", "anchor": "399 million farms worldwide, or 70% of farmers report being affected by climate stress.", "title": "Show the sum, fix farms and farmers", "body": "This mixes farms and farmers in one line, and no source is given for either number. Write the multiplication so a judge can check one factor instead of doubting all three rings.", "after": "Serviceable available market: 70% of farms report climate stress [source], so 570 million x 0.70 = 399 million farms.", "excerpts": ["ent-2025-og-wur-03", "ent-2023-og-leiden-33"], "excerpt_note": "Both winners print the multiplication and put a source on the contested factor.", "ballot": "Has the team shown that their solution is possible, scalable and inventive?"}, {"id": "ent-026", "type": "mistake", "anchor": "there are around 4.7 million organic farms worldwide", "title": "Your smallest market is the wrong farmers", "body": "Here the obtainable market is organic farms. Two sections earlier you argue that over 97% of farmers are conventional, and in section 5.4 you write “Our target market is conventional smallholder farmers”. The Challenges box calls your demographic “smaller, sustainable farmers” again. Pick the group you sell to first and make all three places agree.", "excerpts": [], "ballot": "Has the team identified their first potential customers and their unmet need(s) that are not yet covered by existing solutions?"}, {"id": "ent-027", "type": "praise", "anchor": "Our product is unlikely to be more effective than the current best chemical solutions", "title": "You published a result against yourself", "body": "Few teams write this sentence, and it buys trust for everything around it. It also tells a judge you know what you are competing on, which is speed and precision, not raw strength.", "excerpts": ["ent-2024-ug-padua-19", "ent-2025-og-wur-02"], "excerpt_note": "Both winners state the finding that goes against their own first proposal, then say what they do instead."}, {"id": "ent-028", "type": "mistake", "anchor": "which can price around 80,000 to 10,000NTD for just sensing or just protectant production", "title": "This price range runs backwards", "body": "The range goes from high to low, and NTD is stuck to the number. Also say which competitor each end of the range comes from.", "after": "which cost about 10,000 to 80,000 NTD for sensing alone or protectant production alone ([company], [company])", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "ent-029", "type": "suggest", "anchor": "Our product is the first on the market that is able to respond in real time to the stresses faced by plants", "title": "Add the limit to the first claim", "body": "“First on the market” is the biggest claim on the page and nothing behind it checks the whole market. Put the limit of your knowledge in the same sentence, and name what you searched.", "after": "From the [number] products we reviewed, and to the best of our knowledge, no existing system both senses plant stress and makes the protectant on site.", "excerpts": ["ent-2024-og-utoronto-24", "ent-2025-og-wur-02"], "excerpt_note": "UToronto claims novelty and says in the same breath why it cannot be verified.", "ballot": "Has the team shown that their solution is possible, scalable and inventive?"}, {"id": "ent-030", "type": "mistake", "anchor": "Eco-friendly, zero chemical residue", "title": "Competitor table rows do not line up", "body": "In the rendered table the feature names and the Yes/No answers have slipped out of order, so this feature appears where a company name should be, and only some columns get answers. A judge cannot tell which company has which feature. Rebuild it as one row per feature and one column per company, and give every No a source.", "excerpts": [], "ballot": "Has the team shown that their solution is possible, scalable and inventive?"}, {"id": "ent-031", "type": "praise", "anchor": "Our pricing strategy is designed around seasonal agricultural cash flow", "title": "Price built around when farmers have money", "body": "This opens the pricing section with a fact about your customer, not about your product. The base unit plus seasonal cartridge model then follows from it, which is exactly how a judge wants to read a pricing decision.", "excerpts": ["ent-2024-ug-padua-18"], "excerpt_note": "Padua measures the customer's routine first, then names the design decision that came out of it.", "ballot": "Has the team identified their first potential customers and their unmet need(s) that are not yet covered by existing solutions?"}, {"id": "ent-032", "type": "cut", "anchor": "A possible solution would be a reusable drive and a replaceable cartridge system that aligns best with farmer spending behavior", "title": "Last sentence repeats the second", "body": "The paragraph already said the system is a low-cost reusable base unit with replaceable seasonal cartridges. This sentence says it again and calls it only a possibility, which weakens the earlier decision.", "after": "Delete this sentence.", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "ent-033", "type": "mistake", "anchor": "First commercial pilot programs will launch in 2026", "title": "2026 here, 2027 in the timeline", "body": "This objective puts the first pilots in 2026. The marketing timeline says “Our sales strategy will launch in 2027” under a chart titled 2027 Marketing Strategy, and the roadmap puts the Taiwan field pilot in Year 1.5 to 2.5. Fix the dates so the three agree, and state which year is Year 1.", "excerpts": [], "ballot": "Has the team presented logical product development plans with realistic milestones, timelines, resources and risks?"}, {"id": "ent-034", "type": "mistake", "anchor": "These locations have looser laws on GMOs and AgTech than areas such as Europe or Japan", "title": "The EU cannot be looser than Europe", "body": "The sentence before this one lists the European Union as a first, easier market, then this sentence contrasts those markets with Europe. The roadmap also puts the EU last, in Year 5.5 and later, and the Laws and Regulations page gives the order as Taiwan, European Union, Philippines, Vietnam, Thailand, Indonesia. Three different orders exist across two pages. Agree one order and repeat it.", "excerpts": [], "ballot": "Has the team presented logical product development plans with realistic milestones, timelines, resources and risks?"}, {"id": "ent-035", "type": "mistake", "anchor": "uses a heavy-duty, single-button system with mechanically keyed cartridges to prove the device requires no digital skills", "title": "One button here, a phone app earlier", "body": "Section 2.2 says “The App brings everything together on the farmer’s phone”, with one-click ordering and an AIoT screen for setup. This paragraph says the device needs no digital skills and has one button. Both cannot be the product. If the app is optional, say so in section 2.2.", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "ent-036", "type": "clarify", "anchor": "Once these target farmers see a 10% to 15% increase in crop yields and a 20% to 30% drop in chemical spraying", "title": "Say these are targets, not results", "body": "A judge reading quickly will take these as measured results. Nothing on the page shows a field trial yet, so label them and say where they come from.", "after": "Once our pilot farms reach our target of [number]% higher yield and [number]% less spraying, measured against their own previous season, we will launch a referral discount.", "excerpts": ["sus-2025-og-aalto-02"], "excerpt_note": "Aalto states what stage the project is at before it makes any claim, so the reader knows how to weigh the numbers.", "ballot": "Has the team shown that their solution is possible, scalable and inventive?"}, {"id": "ent-037", "type": "restructure", "anchor": "By developing pilot programs through a 9-month period in total, we get to let farmers have a first hand experience", "title": "Give the two trials, then the total", "body": "The paragraph gives the total first, then the parts, then repeats itself in the last sentence. Reversing the order makes it shorter and clearer.", "after": "We will run a 3-month trial in Q2, then use its feedback to design a 6-month trial across Q3 and Q4. Nine months of use tells us how farmers adapt to the system and what they would change.", "excerpts": [], "ballot": "Has the team presented logical product development plans with realistic milestones, timelines, resources and risks?"}, {"id": "ent-038", "type": "praise", "anchor": "1Concept validation Year 1 · 8/8 done", "title": "Roadmap counts are in the text", "body": "Your roadmap is readable as text, with eight of eight tasks marked done and a duration on every row. Many winning pages hid this in an image, where a judge reading the text cannot check it. This is one of the strongest answers on your page.", "excerpts": ["ent-2024-og-utoronto-25"], "excerpt_note": "UToronto's status block is the same register: dated, countable, no adjectives.", "ballot": "Has the team presented logical product development plans with realistic milestones, timelines, resources and risks?"}, {"id": "ent-039", "type": "clarify", "anchor": "Validate shuttle vector, ccaSR system & MoClo 2.0", "title": "Roadmap labels need a plain line", "body": "The Year 1 rows are written for your lab, not for a business reader. Add one sentence under the chart that says what the year proves, in plain words.", "after": "Year 1 proves the biology works in the lab: the light switch, the three protectants, and the bioreactor that holds the bacteria in.", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "ent-040", "type": "praise", "anchor": "We target 20 to 30% fewer applications per season to directly lower labor and chemical waste", "title": "A pilot target you can be judged on", "body": "This is a number a farmer and a judge can both check at the end of a season, and it is tied to the two things your customer cares about, labour and chemicals. Keep targets written this way.", "excerpts": [], "ballot": "Has the team presented logical product development plans with realistic milestones, timelines, resources and risks?"}, {"id": "ent-041", "type": "cut", "anchor": "followed by the development of stress-tolerant (耐地氧) protectants", "title": "Stray Chinese characters in the roadmap", "body": "The bracketed characters do not translate the words before them and look like a leftover note. iGEM pages are read in English, so remove them or replace them with the term you meant.", "after": "followed by the development of stress-tolerant protectants", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "ent-042", "type": "restructure", "anchor": "separating the physical hardware utility from the biological components into two bundles", "title": "This sentence has no main verb", "body": "The sentence starts with “Thus, to capture...” and never says who does what. Add the subject and the verb.", "after": "We will therefore split the product into two bundles, one with the biology included and one without.", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "ent-043", "type": "clarify", "anchor": "Bundle B will be the standard bioreactor hardware sold standalone alongside a specialized software API", "title": "Does containment still hold for Bundle B?", "body": "Bundle B lets other labs grow their own strains in your hardware. Section 7 builds your whole regulatory case on your own contained strain. Say in one sentence who is responsible for biosafety in Bundle B, because a judge will ask.", "excerpts": [], "ballot": "Has the team considered the positive and negative long-term impacts of their fully developed solution?"}, {"id": "ent-044", "type": "clarify", "anchor": "Machine costs (in USD)", "title": "Is this per unit or for 25 units?", "body": "The row shows quantity 25 and $55,467, so a reader cannot tell whether a machine costs $55,467 or $2,219. The same question applies to the labour row. Add the unit to each row.", "after": "Machine cost: 25 units at $[number] each = $55,467", "excerpts": [], "ballot": "Has the team presented logical product development plans with realistic milestones, timelines, resources and risks?"}, {"id": "ent-045", "type": "mistake", "anchor": "Break even point (units): total fixed cost / (selling price − total variable cost per unit)", "title": "Fixed cost changes between table and sum", "body": "The table above gives the total fixed cost as $89,097. The break-even formula below uses 89,037. Two digits are swapped somewhere. Also, the formula lines show their raw code on the live page, so fix the rendering and give one worked example with your own selling price.", "excerpts": [], "ballot": "Has the team presented logical product development plans with realistic milestones, timelines, resources and risks?"}, {"id": "ent-046", "type": "mistake", "anchor": "if engineered bacteria never leave the sealed device, the system can be classified as “contained-use” equipment", "title": "Laws page calls this route unconfirmed", "body": "Your Laws and Regulations page says it “did not identify a general Taiwan contained-use GMO framework that clearly resolves this specific engineered bacterial production scenario” and treats the status as unconfirmed. This page states the pathway as a fact and builds Phase 2 on it. Match the Laws page wording, or say what new source changed your view.", "after": "Taiwan may allow a contained-use route if engineered bacteria never leave the sealed device. Our Laws and Regulations review could not confirm which framework applies, so we would seek a regulatory consultation before deployment.", "excerpts": [], "ballot": "Has the team presented logical product development plans with realistic milestones, timelines, resources and risks?"}, {"id": "ent-047", "type": "mistake", "anchor": "The engineered bacteria remain physically confined via the 0.22 micrometer hollow-fiber membrane with redundant genetic kill switches", "title": "The kill switch may not exist yet", "body": "Your Human Practices page says Dr. Brophy warned that biological switches mutate and fail, and that the team then decided to “treat physical containment as the primary biosafety mechanism, while evaluating whether an additional kill switch is necessary”. This page, the risk table and the conclusion all describe a built CRISPR kill switch as one of three layers. Say what is built and what is planned.", "after": "The engineered bacteria are held inside by the 0.22 micrometer hollow-fiber membrane and the sealed housing. We are still evaluating whether to add a genetic kill switch.", "excerpts": [], "ballot": "Has the team considered the positive and negative long-term impacts of their fully developed solution?"}, {"id": "ent-048", "type": "suggest", "anchor": "we anticipate securing “absolute containment” certification by end of Phase 2 (Year 2)", "title": "Name who controls this clock", "body": "A timeline becomes evidence when the body that owns the clock is named. At the moment the reader only has your estimate.", "after": "According to [body], this review takes about [number] months, so we expect certification by the end of Year 2.", "excerpts": ["ihp-2025-og-wur-05"], "excerpt_note": "WageningenUR asks the regulator, reports the answer, and lets that answer set the date.", "ballot": "Has the team presented logical product development plans with realistic milestones, timelines, resources and risks?"}, {"id": "ent-049", "type": "mistake", "anchor": "approximately 20 days for fertilizer specification report plus 7 days for government review equals approximately 30 days total", "title": "20 plus 7 is 27, not 30", "body": "The sum does not add up, and the next sentence repeats “within 30 days”. Either correct the arithmetic or say which step you are rounding up and why.", "after": "about 20 days for the fertilizer specification report plus 7 days for government review, so roughly one month to market", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "ent-050", "type": "praise", "anchor": "Registering as a biopesticide would require 3–5 years, multi-season field trials, GLP animal testing", "title": "Two routes compared, choice explained", "body": "This is the best business reasoning on the page. You set two regulatory routes side by side with their costs in time, choose one, and say what you would do later for the antifungal protein. A judge can follow the decision and disagree with it, which is what makes it credible.", "excerpts": ["ent-2023-og-leiden-32"], "excerpt_note": "Leiden lays out the options, gives the reasons, and states the decision that followed.", "ballot": "Has the team presented logical product development plans with realistic milestones, timelines, resources and risks?"}, {"id": "ent-051", "type": "suggest", "anchor": "Genetic containment failure (CRISPR kill switch)", "title": "Tie each risk to a phase", "body": "Your nine risks each have a probability, an impact and a mitigation, which is already good. Add the phase of the roadmap when each risk matters, so the risk list and section 6 line up and a judge can see when you would act.", "after": "Add one line to each risk: Phase: Year 2 field pilot (20–50 units).", "excerpts": [], "ballot": "Has the team presented logical product development plans with realistic milestones, timelines, resources and risks?"}, {"id": "ent-052", "type": "praise", "anchor": "Conduct simple visual/functional tests monthly with farmers or extension officers", "title": "Mitigation someone actually performs", "body": "Most teams write mitigations that nobody could carry out. This one names the task, the schedule and the person. The membrane risk above it is written the same way.", "excerpts": ["ent-2023-ug-tec-30"], "excerpt_note": "Tec-Chihuahua answers each negative with an engineering choice, not a reassurance.", "ballot": "Has the team presented logical product development plans with realistic milestones, timelines, resources and risks?"}, {"id": "ent-053", "type": "cut", "anchor": "ReLeaf represents a transformative approach to agricultural resilience in the face of accelerating climate change", "title": "Open the conclusion on a fact", "body": "The conclusion starts with the vaguest sentence on the page, then repeats the mission for a third time. Your three containment layers and your regulatory choice are the things worth restating.", "after": "ReLeaf makes crop protectants on the farm, only when sensors show stress. Three layers hold the bacteria inside: the membrane, the sealed housing, and the genetic controls we are still testing.", "excerpts": []}, {"id": "ent-054", "type": "mistake", "anchor": "reaching Brazil and Ghana in Phase 4+ for global scaling", "title": "Brazil and Ghana appear only here", "body": "The roadmap in section 6 ends with Thailand and Indonesia, then the European Union and the United States. Brazil and Ghana are named nowhere else on the page, and the phase numbers in this sentence do not match the five phases in the roadmap. Use the roadmap's own markets and phase numbers.", "excerpts": [], "ballot": "Has the team presented logical product development plans with realistic milestones, timelines, resources and risks?"}, {"id": "ent-055", "type": "suggest", "anchor": "we reduce environmental contamination, protect soil microbiomes, and help establish agricultural systems", "title": "The conclusion gives only good news", "body": "The ballot asks for the negative long-term impacts of your finished product, and the page ends with positives only. You already have the material: the price barrier for small farms, cartridge and plastic waste, organic certification, and dependence on one supplier. Add two of them here, each with a cause and what you would do about it.", "after": "Two long-term risks stay with us. A premium price could leave the smallest farms behind, so we plan shared ownership through farmers' associations. Cartridges add plastic waste each season, so we would run a return and refill scheme.", "excerpts": ["ent-2025-og-wur-05", "ent-2023-ug-tec-30"], "excerpt_note": "Both winners write a negative as a chain: cause, who it hurts, and one concrete step.", "ballot": "Has the team considered the positive and negative long-term impacts of their fully developed solution?"}], "excerpts": {"ent-2023-og-leiden-31": {"id": "ent-2023-og-leiden-31", "prize": "entrepreneurship", "team": "Leiden", "year": 2023, "track": "OG", "url": "https://2023.igem.wiki/leiden/entrepreneurship", "section": "Unmet needs & potential customers", "function": "page-opening", "text": "Each year an estimated 2 to 3 million tons of non-degradable, fossil-based plastics are used in agriculture1. These plastics are crucial for safeguarding our crop yields. However, they degrade into microplastics that make their way into our food and our bodies. Research has already found adverse effects of this on human health2. The extent of the harmful effects is not yet known, nonetheless, it is an absolute necessity to PHAse Out non-degradable plastics from agriculture and remove microplastics from our food.\nDespite this urgent need, there are currently no sustainable and cost competitive biodegradable plastics for agricultural use on the market3. Given that biodegradability is a highly attractive characteristic for plastics in agriculture4, this is a large unmet need.", "why_it_works": "Opens on the tonnage, concedes the plastics are useful, states the harm with a citation, then names the gap in one sentence and calls it an unmet need.", "teaches": ["structure", "evidence", "clarity"], "prizeName": "Entrepreneurship"}, "ent-2023-og-leiden-32": {"id": "ent-2023-og-leiden-32", "prize": "entrepreneurship", "team": "Leiden", "year": 2023, "track": "OG", "url": "https://2023.igem.wiki/leiden/entrepreneurship", "section": "Exit strategy", "function": "limitation", "text": "Despite having established that there is an unmet need and a growing market for sustainable PHA, we learned from stakeholders that setting up a PHA production company in the Netherlands ourselves may prove too challenging to be realistically implementable. Firstly, the Dutch bioplastics market is highly competitive, with numerous established players including companies that are already working with PHA5, 6. This creates intense financial and technological demands for market entry, which require financial and operational resources that could be very difficult to access. Moreover, the Netherlands has enforced stringent environmental regulations, particularly in waste management and sustainability, necessitating substantial compliance efforts and investments. As such, we would ideally horizontally merge with a company that already has an established customer base and production facilities.", "why_it_works": "Reports that stakeholders talked the team out of its own plan, gives three reasons, and changes the exit strategy accordingly.", "teaches": ["loop-closing", "evidence", "voice"], "prizeName": "Entrepreneurship"}, "ent-2023-og-leiden-33": {"id": "ent-2023-og-leiden-33", "prize": "entrepreneurship", "team": "Leiden", "year": 2023, "track": "OG", "url": "https://2023.igem.wiki/leiden/entrepreneurship", "section": "Serviceable Obtainable Market", "function": "evidence-with-numbers", "text": "The Serviceable Obtainable Market (SOM) is an estimate of the available revenue from a market sector that a company is able to obtain15. In this case, PHAse Out would be located in the Netherlands which is ideal for capturing the European market. We would first establish ourselves within the Netherlands before attempting to expand further into Europe. The Dutch obtainable market for plastics is currently valued at around $9.6 billion18. Bioplastics make up around 2.5% of the plastic sector19, meaning that there is a current potential of $240 million in the Netherlands for bioplastics. Of these $240 million, 7% of bioplastics used in the Netherlands are PHA20, leaving a total of $16.8 million for the SOM of PHA we could currently achieve expanding into the European market.", "why_it_works": "Chains four sourced percentages down from a national market to a single SOM figure. Each step is a separate sentence with its own reference.", "teaches": ["evidence", "method", "clarity"], "prizeName": "Entrepreneurship"}, "ent-2023-ug-tec-27": {"id": "ent-2023-ug-tec-27", "prize": "entrepreneurship", "team": "Tec-Chihuahua", "year": 2023, "track": "UG", "url": "https://2023.igem.wiki/tec-chihuahua/entrepreneurship", "section": "First Potential Customers", "function": "definition", "text": "After recognizing the presence of small, medium, and large-scale producers, as well as understanding how mastitis affects them, it becomes evident that these individuals, whether under the guidance of a veterinary zootechnician or not, are our ultimate end-users. However, after engaging with them and also with global veterinary pharmaceutical companies like MSD Animal Health and Zoetis, we identified the existence of two distinct customer types.\nWith this we refer to a division between large-scale producers and small to medium-sized producers. Initially, AureoBos will be sold directly to large-scale producers, whereas to reach small and medium-sized ones, an intermediary is required. Thus, the veterinary pharmacies in these regions would also become our clients, serving as the bridge to connect with the smaller producers.", "why_it_works": "Splits one customer group into two by how each buys, and names the intermediary that becomes a customer as a result. The segmentation comes from interviews.", "teaches": ["structure", "clarity", "evidence"], "prizeName": "Entrepreneurship"}, "ent-2023-ug-tec-30": {"id": "ent-2023-ug-tec-30", "prize": "entrepreneurship", "team": "Tec-Chihuahua", "year": 2023, "track": "UG", "url": "https://2023.igem.wiki/tec-chihuahua/entrepreneurship", "section": "Long-term Impacts", "function": "limitation", "text": "Emissions:\n🟥 The production of AureoBos requires cold storage, leading to increased energy consumption and operational costs. Emissions can also be produced by the transportation and disposal of the product.\n⭐We aim to counter this impact by using energy-efficient refrigeration systems and temperature control measures in cold storage facilities as well as exploring newable energy sources like solar energy to power the cold storage, reducing carbon emissions in the near future.", "why_it_works": "A negative impact named with its cause, cold storage and transport, and a mitigation that is an engineering choice rather than a promise.", "teaches": ["limitation", "evidence", "concision"], "prizeName": "Entrepreneurship"}, "ent-2024-og-utoronto-23": {"id": "ent-2024-og-utoronto-23", "prize": "entrepreneurship", "team": "UToronto", "year": 2024, "track": "OG", "url": "https://2024.igem.wiki/utoronto/entrepreneurship.html", "section": "iGEM Entrepreneurship Criteria", "function": "stakeholder-entry", "text": "Through direct engagement with academic leaders, biotechnology KOLs, and our mentors, we've identified and secured our first customers. We're proud to announce that we were approached by Tetsuwan Scientific, a San Francisco-based company, wanting to integrate our platform with their workflow. Our first customers have sent us a proposal for their project, and we're awaiting for our technology to be developed further in order to complete this project. Our future customers include pharmaceutical and biotechnology companies that conduct preclinical discovery as part of their pipeline. Our technology flawlessly integrates and expedites the preclinical discovery process, and projected to cut preclinical costs by ~70%.", "why_it_works": "The first customer is named and the direction of contact is stated: they approached the team. The next sentence then stretches to a \"~70%\" cost cut with no source.", "teaches": ["evidence", "clarity"], "prizeName": "Entrepreneurship"}, "ent-2024-og-utoronto-24": {"id": "ent-2024-og-utoronto-24", "prize": "entrepreneurship", "team": "UToronto", "year": 2024, "track": "OG", "url": "https://2024.igem.wiki/utoronto/entrepreneurship.html", "section": "iGEM Entrepreneurship Criteria", "function": "limitation", "text": "In our discussion with academics, we have confirmed that our usage of AI models to modify phage sequences/DNA alongside our protein interaction validation is at the cutting edge of synthetic biology. Given the proprietary nature of our competitors' technologies, we’re unable to verify how close our approach is to current methods.", "why_it_works": "Claims novelty, then says in the same breath that the claim cannot be verified because rivals keep their methods private. The hedge sits where the claim is.", "teaches": ["voice", "limitation", "evidence"], "prizeName": "Entrepreneurship"}, "ent-2024-og-utoronto-25": {"id": "ent-2024-og-utoronto-25", "prize": "entrepreneurship", "team": "UToronto", "year": 2024, "track": "OG", "url": "https://2024.igem.wiki/utoronto/entrepreneurship.html", "section": "Most Recent Status", "function": "evidence-with-numbers", "text": "The company has successfully completed UofT’s NEST 2024 incubator program and iGEM Startups’ pre-accelerator program. The company will present in Paris, France as part of iGEM Startups’ Showcase. The company has been selected to the “Go-to-Market” stage of UofT’s incubator, unlocking non-dilutive funding, legal, accounting, and other business development resources.", "why_it_works": "Flat, dated, countable: programmes completed, stage reached, what that stage unlocks, headcount as of a date. The register the rest of the page needed.", "teaches": ["concision", "evidence", "voice"], "prizeName": "Entrepreneurship"}, "ent-2024-ug-padua-18": {"id": "ent-2024-ug-padua-18", "prize": "entrepreneurship", "team": "Uni-Padua-IT", "year": 2024, "track": "UG", "url": "https://2024.igem.wiki/uni-padua-it/entrepreneurship", "section": "Customer interview", "function": "loop-closing", "text": "The main issues we identified were related to the monitoring of the filtration process. Currently, water samples are taken approximately every two weeks and sent for analysis using mass spectrometry, which takes several days to yield results. The need for on-site sensors for faster analysis was emphasized, as this would enable prompt action in the event of a malfunction. Based on this feedback, we decided to develop our sensor using Raman technology, eliminating the need for laboratory processing and allowing for rapid, cost-effective water sample analysis in just a few minutes.", "why_it_works": "The need is measured before it is solved: samples every two weeks, results in days. The last sentence names the design decision that came out of that one complaint.", "teaches": ["evidence", "loop-closing", "structure"], "prizeName": "Entrepreneurship"}, "ent-2024-ug-padua-19": {"id": "ent-2024-ug-padua-19", "prize": "entrepreneurship", "team": "Uni-Padua-IT", "year": 2024, "track": "UG", "url": "https://2024.igem.wiki/uni-padua-it/entrepreneurship", "section": "Costs", "function": "evidence-with-numbers", "text": "Furthermore, we need to consider the costs of maintaining conditions suitable for bacterial growth and the expenses for diluting the solutions to ensure compatibility with our Escherichia coli. In this regard, we based our analysis on results obtained from growth tests, which indicate that this bacterium can survive up to a maximum concentration of 0.75 M, equivalent to 150,000 moles of ethanol. Therefore, to the previously considered volume of water, we need to add 91,245.15 L for the dilution of the desorption solution, which amounts to $367.\nAs highlighted from an economic perspective, this alternative does not prove to be cost-effective.", "why_it_works": "Carries a lab growth limit through to litres of dilution water and a dollar figure, then ends by saying the option is not cost-effective. The team publishes the number that kills its own first proposal.", "teaches": ["evidence", "method", "voice"], "prizeName": "Entrepreneurship"}, "ent-2024-ug-padua-21": {"id": "ent-2024-ug-padua-21", "prize": "entrepreneurship", "team": "Uni-Padua-IT", "year": 2024, "track": "UG", "url": "https://2024.igem.wiki/uni-padua-it/entrepreneurship", "section": "Marketing Strategy", "function": "other", "text": "SurPFAS represents more than just a startup; it is a young and innovative movement founded by a group of individuals in their twenties, driven by a clear mission—to eliminate PFAS and increase global awareness of an environmental crisis that demands immediate action.", "why_it_works": "The opening of Marketing Strategy restates the opening of Marketing and Communication almost word for word. Two sections, one paragraph of content.", "teaches": ["concision", "consistency", "structure"], "prizeName": "Entrepreneurship"}, "ent-2025-og-wur-01": {"id": "ent-2025-og-wur-01", "prize": "entrepreneurship", "team": "WageningenUR", "year": 2025, "track": "OG", "url": "https://2025.igem.wiki/wageningenur/entrepreneurship/index.html", "section": "Judging criteria", "function": "stakeholder-entry", "text": "By integrating human practices into the entire decision making process, we made sure that our product, BCoated: Slow Release, is adjusted to market demands. For example, SeedForward told us that a seed coating to deliver insecticides to seeds is in high demand, yet no appropriate solution is available. Based on this information, we started working on developing seed coats that are both sustainable and tailored for the slow release of compounds, such as insecticides. Based on our promising wet lab results, Seed Forward has also expressed interest in helping us perform field trials with BCoated: Slow Release. If these field trials give promising results, we foresee a strong potential customer in Seed Forward. We have scheduled a visit to their headquarters in Osnabrück to discuss the possibilities.", "why_it_works": "Names one company, quotes what it said it lacked, and ties the lab work that followed to that sentence. The customer claim is checkable because the customer is named.", "teaches": ["evidence", "loop-closing", "structure"], "prizeName": "Entrepreneurship"}, "ent-2025-og-wur-02": {"id": "ent-2025-og-wur-02", "prize": "entrepreneurship", "team": "WageningenUR", "year": 2025, "track": "OG", "url": "https://2025.igem.wiki/wageningenur/entrepreneurship/index.html", "section": "Judging criteria", "function": "limitation", "text": "Scalability is, as with many biotechnological processes, a major challenge. We have shown that scaling up in accordance to the ex situ coating method is possible, but that scaling up the in situ method is a greater hurdle. For that reason, BCoated will initially focus on ex situ approaches to build a trusted customer base and develop ourselves as a company. Our R&D will focus on improving our in situ production and downstream processing. This will eventually enable us to enter the market with a novel product line: coated seeds straight from the bioreactor!", "why_it_works": "Admits the harder of its two methods does not scale yet, then says which method the company will sell first because of it. The limitation changes the plan on the page.", "teaches": ["evidence", "voice", "structure"], "prizeName": "Entrepreneurship"}, "ent-2025-og-wur-03": {"id": "ent-2025-og-wur-03", "prize": "entrepreneurship", "team": "WageningenUR", "year": 2025, "track": "OG", "url": "https://2025.igem.wiki/wageningenur/entrepreneurship/index.html", "section": "Serviceable obtainable market", "function": "evidence-with-numbers", "text": "Given that we have shown that our seed coating works as a slow-release system, and that we are based in the Netherlands, our SOM will include the use of slow-release system seed coatings in the Netherlands. It is reported that 33% of all seed coatings focus on slow-release systems13. Additionally, as the Netherlands imports 12% of all seeds in Europe, our SOM will be €640.5 million x 0.12 x 0.33 = €25.36 million12.", "why_it_works": "Every factor in the SOM figure is named, sourced and multiplied out in the open, so a reader can redo the sum or dispute one input.", "teaches": ["evidence", "clarity"], "prizeName": "Entrepreneurship"}, "ent-2025-og-wur-05": {"id": "ent-2025-og-wur-05", "prize": "entrepreneurship", "team": "WageningenUR", "year": 2025, "track": "OG", "url": "https://2025.igem.wiki/wageningenur/entrepreneurship/index.html", "section": "Exclusion of Small-scale Farmers", "function": "limitation", "text": "BCoated: Slow-release is set successfully enter the seed coating market. However, coating the seeds costs money, and thus our seeds will be sold at a premium compared to naked seeds. Due to this premium, our product may become inaccessible for small-scale farmers who have smaller budgets than large-scale farmers. As a result, small-scale farmers will have lower yields, making it even more difficult for them to compete with large-scale farmers. To prevent these social issues from occurring, we will stay in touch with these small-scale farmers and may make special price arrangements with seed coating companies that resell to small scale farmers.", "why_it_works": "A negative impact written as a chain: premium price, lower yields for small farms, wider gap. Then one concrete mitigation, not a reassurance.", "teaches": ["structure", "evidence", "limitation"], "prizeName": "Entrepreneurship"}, "ent-2025-ug-sustech-12": {"id": "ent-2025-ug-sustech-12", "prize": "entrepreneurship", "team": "SUSTech-BIO", "year": 2025, "track": "UG", "url": "https://2025.igem.wiki/sustech-bio/entrepreneurship", "section": "2. Project Background", "function": "evidence-with-numbers", "text": "• Massive death toll: Approximately 3.8 million people die from fungal diseases globally each year (Denning, Lancet Infect Dis 2024), surpassing malaria (0.61 million in 2022) and tuberculosis (1.4 million).\n• Causal attribution gap: >60% of fungal-related deaths in low-income countries remain undercoded (GAFFI 2023), leading to systematic underestimation of the true burden.\n• Disease spectrum: Invasive fungal diseases (IFD) cause 6.5 million annual cases with a 58.5% fatality rate; superficial fungal infections affect 1.3 billion people (WHO 2023), ranking third in prevalence after dental caries and tension-type headache.", "why_it_works": "Each claim carries its source inline, and the death toll is set beside malaria and tuberculosis so the reader can size it without knowing the field.", "teaches": ["evidence", "clarity", "audience-framing"], "prizeName": "Entrepreneurship"}, "ent-2025-ug-sustech-15": {"id": "ent-2025-ug-sustech-15", "prize": "entrepreneurship", "team": "SUSTech-BIO", "year": 2025, "track": "UG", "url": "https://2025.igem.wiki/sustech-bio/entrepreneurship", "section": "5.2.2 Weaknesses", "function": "limitation", "text": "- Uncertainty in protein expression stability: Protein expression stability is a critical issue in biopharmaceuticals, particularly during large-scale production, potentially leading to therapeutic efficacy variations between batches. This instability may not only affect treatment outcomes but also complicate regulatory approval processes.\n- Lack of animal model studies and human trials, resulting in a complete absence of clinical data across all phases.\n- The “bacteria-fighting bacteria” approach conflicts with traditional medication logic: Primary care physicians generally lack confidence in live bacterial therapies.", "why_it_works": "Three weaknesses in three short lines: a process risk, a missing-data risk, and the fact that prescribers do not trust the idea. No hedging around any of them.", "teaches": ["concision", "limitation", "voice"], "prizeName": "Entrepreneurship"}, "ent-2025-ug-sustech-16": {"id": "ent-2025-ug-sustech-16", "prize": "entrepreneurship", "team": "SUSTech-BIO", "year": 2025, "track": "UG", "url": "https://2025.igem.wiki/sustech-bio/entrepreneurship", "section": "Comprehensive Conclusions", "function": "other", "text": "- Mechanism Determination: The exceptional killing efficiency (>99.9%) of Citrobacter watermelonis against Candida auris is primarily attributed to its T6SS secretion system, whose efficacy far surpasses traditional antifungal drugs.\n- Drug Efficiency Limitations: Even at concentrations reaching or exceeding their minimum effective concentrations (MBC/MIC), amorolfine hydrochloride and ketoconazole exhibited a maximum killing efficiency of 98%, indicating an inherent limitation in achieving complete fungal eradication.\n- Application Potential: Aspergillus citreus demonstrates significant potential as a highly effective biocontrol agent, offering a novel approach to address potential resistance or efficacy limitations associated with existing pharmaceutical treatments.", "why_it_works": "The organism changes name three times inside one conclusion block, and none of the names matches the species tested earlier on the page. Results prose has to carry one name throughout.", "teaches": ["consistency", "clarity", "evidence"], "prizeName": "Entrepreneurship"}, "ihp-2024-og-uzh-02": {"id": "ihp-2024-og-uzh-02", "prize": "ihp", "team": "UZurich", "year": 2024, "track": "OG", "url": "https://2024.igem.wiki/uzurich/integrated-human-practices", "section": "Claudia Vaderna - Schweizer Allianz Gentechfrei (Swiss Alliance for a GMO-free agriculture)", "function": "reflection", "text": "The exchange with Claudia Vaderna showed us that we have a responsibility to study the biosafety and the ecological impact of our project. For GMO and non-GMO crops to coexist, our bacterial strain would have to be maintained in the field without the risk of entering neighboring (agro-)ecosystems. It also showed us that a potential product would have to be tested for its impact on soil biodiversity before being applied in the field. We were vindicated in our decision to incorporate a sensing mechanism and kill switch into our bacteria to ensure biosafety, but we learned that we would need to conduct a lot of safety testing before we could responsibly apply a product like RhyzUp.", "why_it_works": "Supplementary case. A critic of GMOs is quoted fairly and her concerns become concrete testing obligations. The team states what was confirmed and what it still owes before release.", "teaches": ["reflection", "consistency", "loop-closing"], "prizeName": "IHP"}, "ihp-2025-og-wur-05": {"id": "ihp-2025-og-wur-05", "prize": "ihp", "team": "WageningenUR", "year": 2025, "track": "OG", "url": "https://2025.igem.wiki/wageningenur/human-practices/index.html", "section": "#7.2 Insights from regulatory institutions", "function": "evidence-with-numbers", "text": "We spoke to RIVM ( Samia Ouhajji, Casper Jongerkrijg, Dr. Jeroen Roelfsema ) to understand whether our various tuned BC coatings with different properties would require separate approvals. Samia Ouhajji explained that chemicals can be grouped for approval, and BC can be registered as a chemical. This simplifies the approval procedure for our product. However, each toxic protein module still requires its own independent risk assessment, ensuring that there will be no off-target effects. That means some of our functionalisations would require a separate assessment. They also emphasised the 0.9% GMO DNA threshold in the final products, regardless of whether the cells are dead or alive. This will impact the downstream processing of our product.", "why_it_works": "Says why the team asked, what the regulator answered, and which part of the product each answer touches. The 0.9% threshold is tied directly to a consequence for downstream processing.", "teaches": ["evidence", "loop-closing", "assumed-knowledge"], "prizeName": "IHP"}, "sus-2025-og-aalto-02": {"id": "sus-2025-og-aalto-02", "prize": "sustainability", "team": "Aalto-Helsinki", "year": 2025, "track": "OG", "url": "https://2025.igem.wiki/aalto-helsinki/sustainability", "section": "Our project in relation to Sustainable Development", "function": "audience-framing", "text": "Since we have accomplished only the preliminary stages of the development of AbsorBuddy, many questions remain open and much work remains to be done, however, we have transparently documented our work, including its potentials and limitations. In line with the iGEM requirements for the Sustainability Special Prize, we are showcasing on this page our critical reflections on how our project fits with global efforts for Sustainable Development, rather than promising that our work and solution are sustainable in every way. We hope that the reflections that emerged from our work, can be built upon by future iGEM teams and the broader research community.", "why_it_works": "Says what stage the project is at and what the page will claim before any goal is discussed. The reader knows how to weigh everything that follows, and the team cannot be caught overclaiming later.", "teaches": ["voice", "limitation"], "prizeName": "Sustainability"}}};
